cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite the paper."
title: >-
  Higher Scores, Not A Better Model: A Large Multiple-Choice Lift That Is
  Mostly Inducible, Off-Axis, and Does Not Transfer to Generation
authors:
  - family-names: Cascardo
    given-names: Sebastián
    email: sebacascardo@gmail.com
    affiliation: Independent Researcher
abstract: >-
  Contrastive-correctness parameter-efficient fine-tuning of the LoFiT/ITI family
  buys a large cold multiple-choice lift on a frontier 31B instruction model
  (ARC-Challenge +35.0, HellaSwag +17.0, Winogrande +19.0, TruthfulQA-mc1 +37.25).
  We ask what that lift is and answer with a quantified causal attribution: much of
  it is inducible without the contrastive objective, the recovery is off the
  measured one-dimensional correctness direction, and it does not transfer to
  generation. A benchmark gain of this kind should not be read as a capability gain.
keywords:
  - mechanistic interpretability
  - parameter-efficient fine-tuning
  - benchmark evaluation
  - language models
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/seba-cascardo/higher-scores-not-a-better-model"
type: software
